# Shell scripts MUST check out with LF on every platform.
#
# They are executed by bash — including Git Bash on Windows and on the CI runners, where every
# workflow step uses `shell: bash`. A CR at end of line breaks the shebang (`bad interpreter:
# .../bash^M`) and, more insidiously, string comparisons: a trailing \r makes `[ "$RUNNER_OS" =
# 'Windows' ]` false on Windows. This repo's usual working-tree setting is core.autocrlf=true, so
# without this rule the conversion is decided by whatever git config a machine or runner image
# happens to have.
*.sh text eol=lf

# SQL files too. The macro files under dotnet/Fabricator.Functions/Macros are embedded into the
# assembly as resources, so their line endings end up in the SQL a macro produces: with
# core.autocrlf=true a Windows checkout would ship CRLF and a Linux one LF, and
# decision_render_tsql would generate different T-SQL text depending on where it was built.
*.sql text eol=lf

# sqllogictest files likewise: an expected value spanning several lines would carry a CR on a
# CRLF checkout and stop matching (verify_decision_render_tsql does).
*.test text eol=lf
